  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277438",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
